From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 10:55:42 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] tcp_input: refuse a connect to port 0 of the host with a
 reset

The previous topic leaves a guest SYN to port 0 unanswered, which is
what an unreachable destination does on a real network. The gateway
and broadcast addresses are not remote: SLiRP translates them to the
host's loopback, and a host that received the SYN would answer for a
port nothing listens on with a reset. On Linux, a connect to loopback
port 0 fails with ECONNREFUSED.

Answer such a connect with RST|ACK, as for any refused connection.
Destinations that are not translated to the host stay unanswered.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/tcp_input.c | 31 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/tcp_input.c b/src/tcp_input.c
index 38a841a1ad84af86220d1f53412789a4766d558e..3e80126029ea25b40f3cb72db003a5989137667e 100644
--- a/src/tcp_input.c
+++ b/src/tcp_input.c
@@ -166,6 +166,30 @@ present:
     return (flags);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Whether the connection goes to the host itself: the gateway and broadcast
+ * addresses are translated to its loopback, where a host stack that
+ * received the SYN would answer for a port nothing listens on.
+ */
+static bool tcp_connects_to_host(struct socket *so)
+{
+    struct sockaddr_storage addr = so->fhost.ss;
+
+    if (sotranslate_out(so, &addr) < 0) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    switch (addr.ss_family) {
+    case AF_INET:
+        return ((struct sockaddr_in *)&addr)->sin_addr.s_addr ==
+               loopback_addr.s_addr;
+    case AF_INET6:
+        return in6_equal(&((struct sockaddr_in6 *)&addr)->sin6_addr,
+                         &in6addr_loopback);
+    default:
+        return false;
+    }
+}
+
 /*
  * TCP input routine, follows pages 65-76 of the
  * protocol specification dated September, 1981 very closely.
@@ -636,8 +660,13 @@ findso:
                  * nothing from an unreachable host, and the guest would
                  * retransmit until it gave up. Answering with an ICMP error
                  * instead failed the connect at once. Leave the SYN
-                 * unanswered; tcp_close and m_free below discard it.
+                 * unanswered; tcp_close and m_free below discard it. The
+                 * host itself is there to answer, and refuses port 0.
                  */
+                if (tcp_connects_to_host(so)) {
+                    tcp_respond(tp, ti, m, ti->ti_seq + 1, (tcp_seq)0,
+                                TH_RST | TH_ACK, af);
+                }
             } else {
                 switch (af) {
                 case AF_INET:
